note.text:
Before colonisation Cameroon had two main kinds of society.
State-like (centralised) societies had a central ruler with great power, helped by a court and officials:
• Fulani lamidates in the north (Ngaoundéré, Garoua, Maroua), ruled by a lamido;
• the Bamum kingdom, ruled by a sultan (mfon), such as King Njoya (late 19th–early 20th century), who invented the Bamum script;
• the Grassfields chiefdoms (Nso, Bali, Mankon, Bafut, Bamileke chiefdoms), ruled by a fon or chief with secret societies and councils;
• the Mandara kingdom and the Kotoko city-states in the north.
Features: a hereditary ruler, a palace and court, officials and councils, an army, tribute and taxes, laws and courts, and royal rituals.
